### semantics: add `fail` function to the SemanicAnalyser


This allows for compilation failure in non-pruned paths. For example,
consider a `static_assert` macro:

```
macro static_assert(v, msg) {
  if (typeof(v) != typeof(true)) {
    fail("unable to evaluate compile-time assert");
  } else if (!v) {
    fail(msg);
  }
}
```

Signed-off-by: Adin Scannell <amscanne@meta.com>

bpftrace crashes with a SEGFAULT when printing a linear histogram that contains only values in the final overflow bucket.

**Steps to reproduce:**
(records a 150B write into an `lhist` with a max 100 range)

```bash
# bash -c "sleep 1; exec dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/null bs=150 count=1 status=none" &
# pid=$!
# build/src/bpftrace -e "tracepoint:syscalls:sys_enter_write /pid == $pid/ { @bytes = lhist(args->count, 0, 100, 10); }"
Attached 1 probe
^C

@bytes:
Segmentation fault
```

The root cause is that the `TextEmitter<Value::Histogram>::emit` logic does not handle this overflow-only scenario.

The histogram building logic `build_linear_histogram`, contains an optimization to skip leading empty buckets.

```gdb
Thread 1 "bpftrace" hit Breakpoint 1, bpftrace::build_linear_histogram (
    values=std::vector of length 1002, capacity 1002 = {...}, min=0, max=100, step=10)
(gdb) list
400       output::Value::Histogram hist;
401       auto [min_index, max_index] = indices(values);
          ...
405       if (min_index != 0) {
406         hist.lower_bound = lhist_index_label(min + ((min_index - 1) * step), step);
407       }
408
409       for (int i = min_index; i <= max_index; i++) {
410         // The final bucket represents values over the label, and we encode
411         // this into the histogram structure by simply omitting the label.
412         if (min + (i * step) <= max) {
413           hist.labels.push_back(lhist_index_label(min + (i * step), step));
414         }
415         hist.counts.push_back(values.at(i));
416       }
417       return hist;
(gdb) next ...
417       return hist;
(gdb) p hist
$1 = {lower_bound = std::optional = {[contained value] = {
      variant = std::variant [index 2] = {100}}},
  labels = std::vector of length 0, capacity 0,
  counts = std::vector of length 1, capacity 1 = {1}}
```

This results in a `Value::Histogram` with a `lower_bound` but an empty `labels` vector, as shown in the GDB output.

The crash occurs at `TextEmitter<Value::Histogram>::emit`, since it assumes that if `lower_bound` exists, `labels` is not empty, and it accesses `labels[0]` without a check.

```cpp
struct TextEmitter<Value::Histogram> {
  static void emit(std::ostream &out, const Value::Histogram &hist)  {
    ...
    for (size_t i = 0; i < hist.counts.size() || i < hist.labels.size(); i++) {
      ...
      if (i == 0) {
        if (!hist.lower_bound) {
          ...
        } else if (single_value(*hist.lower_bound, hist.labels[i])) {
          header << "[";
          TextEmitter<Primitive>::emit(header, *hist.lower_bound);
          header << "]";
        } else {
          header << "[";
          TextEmitter<Primitive>::emit(header, *hist.lower_bound);
          header << ", ";
          TextEmitter<Primitive>::emit(header, hist.labels[i]);
          header << ")";
        }
```


## Proposal

`TextEmitter<Value::Histogram>::emit` should be enhanced to correctly interpret this overflow only situation.
The proposed change adds a condition to `src/output/text.cpp` to detect when `lower_bound` exists but `labels` is empty, and then formats the output correctly.

```cpp
// in template <> struct TextEmitter<Value::Histogram>

if (i == 0) {
  if (!hist.lower_bound) {
    ...
  } else if (hist.labels.empty()) { // Oveflow only case
    header << "[";
    TextEmitter<Primitive>::emit(header, *hist.lower_bound);
    header << ", ...)";
  } else if (single_value(*hist.lower_bound, hist.labels[i])) {
    ...
}
```


